1. job vs worker
    - a job is a unit of work that needs to be executed asynchronously. worker - is a process that continuously listens
    to a queue and executes jobs.

2. What happens if a worker crashes during a job?
    - if a worker crashes during a job, the job may be left uncompleted and is typically retried later by the queue
    system. And this is why jobs should be designed to be idempotent, since the same job can be executed multiple times.

3. At least once delivery
    - it guarantees that a job will be executed at least once, but thus it may be executed multiple times, because of
    this system must be designed to handle duplicate execution safely.

4. Retries
    - it is a mechanism that automatically re-executes failed jobs to handle temporary errors. However, because of this
    jobs should be designed to be idempotent to avoid duplicate side effects.

5. Queue backlog
    - it is a number of jobs waiting in a queue that have not yet been processed. it occurs when jobs are produced faster
    than they are processed.

6. Scaling workers
    - it means increasing the number or capacity of queue workers so that jobs are processed faster and the system can
    handle a higher workload. Horizontal scaling - simply run more worker processes in parallel. Vertical scaling - give
    workers more resources (CPU, RAM)